<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=sulfuric-acid">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Chemistry</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sulfuric acid</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">before c. 1100</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Oil of vitriol, Sulphuric acid</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sulfuric acid, called oil of vitriol by medieval alchemists, is a strong acid first made by distilling vitriols, the hydrated sulfates of metals. The Jabirian authors and Abu Bakr al-Razi distilled vitriols, and al-Razi may have made the acid unknowingly; according to Ahmad Y. al-Hassan, an anonymous Garshuni <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> from before c. 1100 holds three recipes for it. European works first allude to it in the thirteenth century. Joshua Ward of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> began large-scale production in 1736, John Roebuck’s lead chamber process of 1746 in Birmingham industrialised it, and Peregrine Phillips patented the contact process in 1831, by which nearly all of it is made today.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sulfuric-acid.jpg" alt="Sulfuric acid" width="330" height="466" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: W. Oelen · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sulphuric_acid_96_percent_extra_pure.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The study of vitriols, the hydrated sulfates of various metals from which sulfuric acid can be derived, began in ancient times. The Sumerians had a list of types of vitriol classified by colour, and the Greek physician Dioscorides and the Roman naturalist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pliny-the-elder">Pliny the Elder</a> discussed its origin and properties. Metallurgical uses are recorded in the Hellenistic alchemical works of Zosimos of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhmim">Panopolis</a>, and Ibn Sina and Muhammad ibn Ibrahim al-Watwat included vitriol in their mineral lists. Medieval European alchemists called the acid oil of vitriol because it was prepared by roasting green vitriol in an iron retort.</p><p>By the 16th century a method called oleum sulphuris per campanam, oil of sulfur by the bell, was known: it burned sulfur under a glass bell in moist weather. It was very inefficient, since Gesner reported that 5 pounds of sulfur gave less than 1 ounce of acid, and most alchemists, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isaac-newton">Isaac Newton</a> among them, did not consider it equivalent to oil of vitriol.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Sulfuric acid is a mineral acid of sulfur, oxygen and hydrogen with the formula H2SO4, a colourless, odourless, viscous liquid that mixes with water. Pure sulfuric acid does not occur naturally because it absorbs water vapour readily. The concentrated acid is a strong oxidant with a powerful dehydrating property and is highly corrosive. Dilution releases considerable heat, so the acid is added to water and not the reverse. The permanent clouds of Venus are made of concentrated sulfuric acid.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>In 1746 John Roebuck of Birmingham adapted the sulfur method to lead-lined chambers, which were stronger, cheaper and larger than glass vessels. This lead chamber process made industrial production possible and remained the standard for almost two centuries. Refinements by Joseph Louis Gay-Lussac and John Glover raised the concentration to 78 percent, but dyes and other processes needed a stronger acid, which in the 18th century could be made only by dry <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/distillation">distillation</a> of minerals such as pyrite, at too great an expense for large-scale use.</p><p>In 1831 the British vinegar merchant Peregrine Phillips patented the contact process, a far more economical way to make sulfur trioxide and concentrated acid, by which nearly all of it is now made. In the early and mid 19th century vitriol plants stood at Prestonpans in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shropshire">Shropshire</a> and in the Lagan Valley in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-antrim">County Antrim</a>, where it bleached linen faster than the lactic acid of sour milk. Its dominant modern use is in making phosphoric acid for fertilizers.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sulfuric acid” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>before c. 1100</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>The medieval Islamic world</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Al-Razi may have made it without knowing in a recipe of his Book of Secrets. An Arabic treatise with a recipe for it, falsely attributed to Ja'far al-Sadiq, is dated to the thirteenth century by Julius Ruska but earlier by al-Hassan.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/distillation">Distillation<span>c. 1200 BCE</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/alchemy">Alchemy<span>1st century CE</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=sulfuric-acid">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
